Occupational Asthma
Respiratory System
Work-related wheezing, Improvement on weekends, Cross-shift PEFR variability

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: RPS-033
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe exacerbation of wheezing and chest tightness occurring immediately upon returning home from a work shift, requiring urgent bronchodilation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported an adult patient with no childhood history of asthma experiencing severe dyspnea, wheeze, and chest tightness that peaks violently after their factory shift. Urgent assessment was required to reverse acute bronchospasm and evaluate the need for systemic steroids before the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: Every Friday evening my chest completely locks up and whistles, but by Sunday afternoon I feel totally fine again until Monday hits.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports adult-onset wheezing, chest tightness, and dyspnea tightly correlated to the workplace (a furniture manufacturing plant). They describe symptoms improving significantly during weekends or holidays away from work, and worsening progressively throughout the workweek. Tonight, they experienced a late-phase asthmatic reaction occurring 4 hours after leaving the workplace, resulting in a severe dry cough and wheeze. They report a history of exposure to known sensitizers (wood dust and isocyanates in varnishes). They also note associated allergic rhinitis and conjunctivitis while on the factory floor.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently restricted to a chair due to breathlessness.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of sudden severe exacerbation if workplace exposure continues; potential loss of livelihood.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 mmHg | HR: 105 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 24 breaths/min | SpO2: 94% on room air | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Alert, anxious, mild respiratory distress, audible wheeze from the end of the bed.
Physical Exam: Chest auscultation reveals a prolonged expiratory phase with widespread polyphonic expiratory wheeze bilaterally. Air entry is fair but reduced globally. Eye exam shows mild residual bilateral conjunctival injection. The patient notes that their physical examination during extended time off (e.g., a recent 2-week vacation) is completely normal.
Environmental Observations: Patient's work clothes (covered in fine dust) are sitting in the laundry basket nearby.
Medication Reconciliation: Recently purchased over-the-counter salbutamol inhaler used 4 times with only partial relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Occupational Asthma - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Typical Atopic Asthma - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The explicit adult-onset nature, the strict temporal relationship to the workweek, and complete resolution during holidays, which strongly point to an occupational sensitizer.

Acute Bronchitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The absence of fever, absence of purulent sputum, and the chronic, cyclical nature of the symptoms linked to shifts.

Hypersensitivity Pneumonitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Presence of classic expiratory wheeze (bronchospasm) rather than fine inspiratory crackles ("squeaks") and systemic flu-like symptoms.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Occupational exposure)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, late-phase exacerbation of occupational asthma, triggered by established workplace sensitizers (wood dust/isocyanates). The strict pattern of work-related wheezing, progressive worsening through the week, and complete improvement on weekends is virtually pathognomonic. The current late-phase reaction (hours after leaving work) has caused significant bronchospasm and borderline hypoxia. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period posed a high risk of progressive airway narrowing and the need for emergency department presentation. An urgent after-hours home visit was strictly necessary to administer high-dose nebulized bronchodilators, initiate systemic corticosteroids, and establish immediate workplace avoidance strategies.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered nebulized Salbutamol 5mg and Ipratropium Bromide 500mcg, resulting in marked improvement in air entry. Prescribed oral Prednisolone 40mg daily for 5 days. Initiated a peak flow diary.
Positive Behavioral Change: Record your peak flow reading exactly three times a day (before work, during shift, before bed) in the provided logbook; documenting this specific cross-shift variability provides the undeniable objective proof required for your specialist to formally diagnose the workplace trigger and support your occupational health claim.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Provided a Peak Expiratory Flow Rate (PEFR) meter.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review on Monday morning to arrange formal spirometry and referral to a Respiratory/Occupational Physician.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department via 000 if there is no relief from the reliever inhaler after 10 puffs, if the patient struggles to speak in sentences, or if the wheezing rapidly returns after the nebulizer wears off.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: A formal diagnosis of Occupational Asthma requires specialized serial lung function testing and specialist review to prove the exact workplace trigger.
Clinical Course: The acute spasm is reversed, and the steroids will reduce the swelling, but returning to the identical unmitigated workplace environment on Monday will trigger another severe attack.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call 000 immediately if you feel your chest locking up entirely, or if you feel too exhausted to breathe.
PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: A specific chemical or dust at your job has caused your immune system to become hyper-allergic; your airways are now violently swelling up and spasming hours after you breathe it in, requiring the steroids to shut down the reaction.
Actionable Steps: You must wear an appropriate, fitted P2/N95 respirator mask at all times on the factory floor starting Monday; preventing the physical inhalation of the dust/fumes is the only way to stop your lungs from aggressively spasming while we arrange specialist testing.

